<div class="mx-auto max-w-md pb-12">
  <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold text-gray-900">
      <%= @workout[:date].strftime('%A %b %-d') %>
    </h1>
    <%# "record" was the noun -- the page that records this session -- and read as the verb,
        which on a gym screen is an instruction to start recording. It is also where you go
        when you have stopped lifting, so it says that instead (#216).

        It only navigated, though, and #218 is what that cost: nothing in the app could say
        a session was over, so a day finished with three of ten sets done looked exactly
        like one still under way -- to the workouts list, and to an assistant reading the
        history through MCP, which called it in progress. The words were already right. Now
        the control does what they say, and lands on the record either way, which is where
        the link went before.

        A button rather than a link because it writes, and a form rather than htmx because
        this is the one control on the screen whose whole job is to leave it. Styled as the
        link it replaces: it sits beside an h1 as a quiet way out, not as the thing the
        page is for -- that is Done, five times over, below. %>
    <form action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/session/finish" method="post">
      <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/session/finish") %>
      <button type="submit" class="min-h-11 text-sm text-gray-500 underline hover:text-lime-500">
        <%= @workout[:finished_at] ? 'finished' : 'finish workout' %>
      </button>
    </form>
  </div>

  <div id="session-body">
    <%== render('workouts/_session_body') %>
  </div>
</div>

<%# The lifts are a horizontal scroller now, and htmx replaces the whole of #session-body
    after every tap. An innerHTML swap destroys the scroller along with everything else in
    there, so its scrollLeft goes with it: without this, finishing a set on lift three
    drops the lifter back on lift one, every set, all session. That is the one thing that
    would have made the panels unusable.

    hx-preserve is the obvious answer and it is the wrong one: it keeps the old element,
    which means it keeps the old element's children, so the set that was just completed
    would never redraw. Re-rendering the panels is the entire point of the swap. What has
    to survive is one number, and only that.

    It is a listener on document.body rather than on the scroller because htmx events
    bubble, so this binds once when the page loads and keeps working across every swap.
    Binding to the scroller itself -- or writing the gesture in JS at all -- would mean
    re-binding after each swap to an element that no longer exists, which is the class of
    bug that only shows up mid-session with a bar in your hands.

    The offset is put back raw rather than recomputed from a lift index. Every panel is
    exactly the scroller's own width, so the offset before the swap already names a snap
    point, and the browser re-snaps to it anyway if a swipe was in flight.

    None of this runs without JavaScript and none of it needs to. The stylesheet is a
    stylesheet since #188 -- it was the Tailwind play CDN, a script, when this was written,
    so the claim used to be that JS off meant no flex row and no snapping at all. It is
    compiled now, so with JS off the panels are still a real horizontal snap scroller; what
    is lost is the offset restore below, and the forms post normally and the route
    redirects, which is the page this screen has always degraded to. %>
<%# The second thing in here is #212: the progress bar did not move when you swiped. It
    could not -- it is rendered from what is completed, and swiping completes nothing -- so
    a bar sitting still above a screen that had just changed read as a bar that had stopped
    working. markCurrentLift marks the segment belonging to the lift you are looking at, so
    the one bar says both how much of the session is done and where in it you are.

    Which panel that is comes from the scroll offset over the panel width, both read off the
    scroller itself rather than from a stored index, so it stays right when the width changes
    under it -- a rotation, or the 640px breakpoint where the panels become a stack. It is
    called on scroll, after every swap, and once on load, which is every way the answer can
    change.

    The scroll listener is on document in the capture phase because scroll events do not
    bubble: a listener on the scroller itself would have to be re-bound after every htmx
    swap destroys it, which is the same trap the offset restore above avoids. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    var offset = 0;

    function markCurrentLift() {
      var panels = document.getElementById('lift-panels');
      var segments = document.querySelectorAll('[data-lift-segment]');
      if (!panels || !panels.clientWidth || !segments.length) { return; }
      var current = Math.round(panels.scrollLeft / panels.clientWidth);
      for (var i = 0; i < segments.length; i++) {
        segments[i].setAttribute('data-current', i === current ? 'true' : 'false');
      }
    }

    document.addEventListener('scroll', function (event) {
      if (event.target && event.target.id === 'lift-panels') { markCurrentLift(); }
    }, true);

    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:beforeSwap', function () {
      var panels = document.getElementById('lift-panels');
      if (panels) { offset = panels.scrollLeft; }
    });
    document.body.addEventListener('htmx:afterSwap', function () {
      var panels = document.getElementById('lift-panels');
      if (panels) { panels.scrollLeft = offset; }
      markCurrentLift();
    });

    markCurrentLift();
  })();
</script>
